{"type":"message","id":"45ad368d","parentId":"4bbb52eb","timestamp":"2026-10-20T04:10:26.560Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`sort_a` is faster: about 2.5× quicker than `sort_b` on 100k random ints. `RESULT.md` now contains `faster: sort_a`.\n\n| function | best of 3 runs |\n|---|---|\n| `sort_a` | 0.179 s |\n| `sort_b` | 0.445 s |\n\nThe input was 100k random ints in [0, 1e9], with seed 0. Before timing, I checked that both functions return the same list as `sorted(xs)`.\n\n`sort_b` calls `bisect.insort` once per element. Each insert has to shift part of the list, so the total work grows with the square of the input size, against n log n for `sort_a`'s merge sort. At 100k elements `sort_b` stays reasonably close because that shifting runs as fast C code, but the gap will keep widening as inputs get bigger."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":275,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":37609,"totalTokens":37886,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":37609}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792469422684,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011CgCqLy3TUk7qhhpWboQ9n","duration":3856.537708,"ttft":3848.9031959999993,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":37611,"nonMessageTokens":18444}}}
